notes='''### 044-N1 — Least fineness and preorder proof

The proof shows the constructed topology refines τ, induces τ′_0, and is less fine than every allowed extension τ″. A margin says this is the first use of T being closed in S. It then starts the preorder proof, recalls the closure characterization, disposes of the two same-part cases and the impossible U-to-T case, and reduces to y in U. The NB says x need not lie in T. Manuscript page 43; no date.

### 044-N2 — Literal slips and decisive signs

The comparison of restricted topologies reads τ′|U≠τ|U: its crop shows two horizontal strokes cut by a clear upright/slant. It is retained despite the equality required by the argument. Later the closure of y is related to T by membership ∈, not inclusion. The last equivalence has a clear prime under both order signs, though the intended right-hand order is the original τ. The reminder also says x,y∈T, not an inferred S. “Il induit” follows “Cette topologie”; that agreement shift stays. The raised restriction has a wave above T and a separate comma-like high mark beside τ″; their meaning is not silently supplied.

### 044-N3 — Targeted recheck of least fineness

For every allowed extension τ″, T is τ″-closed because it is τ-closed and τ″ is finer. B, closed in the induced τ′_0 on T, is therefore τ″-closed in S. The original closure cl_τ(X∩U) is τ-closed, hence τ″-closed. Their finite union X is τ″-closed. This directly checks the displayed least-fineness argument. It does not validate the literal ≠ sign or the self-comparison at the foot; those are reported as ink-level slips.'''
